Q: Python casting string without knowing its type A: <h1>1</h1>
<p>You could do a series of try parses. If successful, you have a type. Whether it is the original type that was cast into a string, you wouldn't know.</p>
<p>pseudo code:</p>
<pre><code>def getUnderlyingType(string value)
{
    if(decimal.tryParse(value, out dec)) 
    {
        return dec;
    }

    if(int.tryParse(value, out inty))
    {
        return inty;
    }

    if(bool.tryParse(value, out booly)
    {
        return booly;
    }

    // etc...
}
</code></pre>
<h1>2</h1>
<p>If you are controlling the storing of these strings in the first place, you could create a dictionary or similar to store the string with its underlying type.</p>
<p>pseudo code:</p>
<pre><code>decimal foo = 4.5;
Dictionary&lt;string, T&gt; casts = new();
cast.Add(foo.toString(), typeof(foo));
</code></pre>
